From ee271c4d3b2dec79e65ea2cfac19ea17c3b2d7b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MiaAI-Lab <mia.ailab1@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 18:28:28 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 61/63] glm cuda: a paired step's twin holds no tap rows and
 shares the first chunk's exchange room, 152 MiB a rank

The pair's twin took 296 MiB a rank at TP=2 beside DFlash2: its own rows (152 MiB), its own exchange
rooms (64 MiB) and a chunk of tap rows (80 MiB). On the production plan (4 streams, a 864,256-token
window on 1,692 KV pages, reserve 3.5 GiB) the room past the pool's window and the streams' fixed parts
is 188.75 MB, so the twin dropped a stream and left the prompt cache 0 bytes, which turns the store off,
its pinned host tier with it. Now the twin takes 158.9 MB and the pool, the streams and the host tier
stay; only the device prompt pages shrink (to about 28 MB).

- Tap rows: a paired step's second chunk taps into DFlash2's own buffers. If the first chunk taps none,
  b's rows go to taps_prefill as an unpaired chunk's do. If both reach the last window, b's rows go to
  the drafter's context input past a's kept rows (dflash pairTaps): idle through the pass, and the a+b
  rows in the window are at most one window (2,048 = MAX_CTX). a's ingest copies its rows in before
  them; b's (promptIn) reads its own in place, so ingest skips that copy. Same rows, same N, same
  kernels as a chunk at a time.
- Exchange room: with split exchanges (TF_GLM_PREFILL_REDUCE=split or ring, the default) only the side
  stream touches a piece's room: partials write it, glue reads it. That stream posts both chunks' sites
  in step order, so the twin takes the first chunk's room (Pair.own_room false). A whole piece fills its
  room on the main stream, so a step pairs only where every piece of both chunks splits
  (prompt_pair.piecesSplit; e.g. 769 rows in 3 pieces end in one of 1 row). pairedAt and every rank's
  prefill check it, and pairBody refuses otherwise. Gather mode keeps a room of its own.
- DFlash2's context run: the second chunk's ingest (formerly ingestPromptFrom) skipped mem2's
  Slot.first/end bookkeeping, so after a paired step the ring's run ended at a's end. A state kept at
  the next mark then carried no drafter context (keptFrom null), and a resume drafted from the mark.
  Every prompt ingest now notes its run (dflash.noteRun) whichever buffer its taps were in.

Host test (test-glm-snapshots-host): a 7,144-id prompt paired at chunks 2 and 3 leaves the same runs,
first and end as four chunks one at a time, reads chunk 3's taps in place and gives the context input
back; a chunk partly before the window, or past MAX_CTX, is refused in place. A unit test covers the
piece rule.

